  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041143/assets/b2fbcc246230bd88e58d7928384a6d09966e37af63879775470fb58dd5005582.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At the time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=4.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=7?$",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=4,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=4+(-3)=1\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=1.$",
